00:01:34.000 --> 00:02:13.000
ambiguous loss in the 1970s. Coming from the discipline of family therapy, she originally implied the term to family's mourning a loved one whose loss was in some way unresolved, like a soldier missing in war. In the years since, she and other researchers have expanded the scope of ambiguous loss, using it as a framework through which to view any number of personal, communal, or global losses, from the pandemic to racial trauma to climate change. Her new book, The Myth of Closure, ambiguous loss in a time of pandemic and change addresses these topics and more. Thank you for joining us, Dr. Boss. It's my pleasure.
“模棱两可的损失”（ambiguous loss）这一术语，时间是在20世纪70年代。她最初将这一术语应用于家庭治疗领域，指代那些因亲人以某种方式处于“未解决”的失踪状态（如战争中失踪的士兵）而哀悼的家庭。此后多年，她和其他研究人员扩展了“模棱两可的损失”的范畴，将其作为一个框架，用以审视各种个人、社区或全球性的损失，从大流行病到种族创伤，再到气候变化。她的新书《终结的神话：大流行与变革时代的模棱两可的损失》探讨了这些话题以及更多内容。感谢您参加我们的节目，博斯博士。这是我的荣幸。

