that is the breathing ends, versus the attachment. So I'm really coming out of attachment theory of Volvey, which says that once you make an attachment to someone or something, I couldn't even talk about someone's home. you make an attachment that even if that thing is no longer with you, if the person has stopped breathing or if you've lost that home or that pet, you can still feel attached to them. You can still say, I love that.
即呼吸停止，与依恋之间的区别，我们更有可能认识到这一点。所以我真的源于鲍尔比的依恋理论，该理论认为，一旦你与某人或某物建立了依恋，我甚至无法谈论某人的家，你建立了一种依恋，即使那个东西不再和你在一起，如果那个人已经停止呼吸，或者你失去了那个家或那只宠物，你仍然可以感到依恋。你仍然可以说：“我爱那个。”

22
00:15:51.000 --> 00:16:31.000
That was my favorite place. I love that person. That was my favorite pet of all times. I love that pet. I miss them. you know they're gone. And so we call that continuing bonds that came first from the edited book by K-L-A-S-S and and other his colleagues, that continuing bonds is what is normal grief. Getting over it is an artificial idea put upon, I think, of the people who are grieving in a culture
“那是我最喜欢的地方。我爱那个人。那是我有史以来最喜欢的宠物。我爱那只宠物。我想念他们。”你知道他们走了。所以我们称之为“持续的纽带”，这首先来自克拉斯（Klass）和他同事编辑的书，即“持续的纽带”才是正常的悲伤。“走出来”是一个强加给悲伤者的虚假观念，我认为，在一个

23
00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:55.000
that has no patience for suffering. We might be that culture. You wrote in your book about your own experience of loss from your early childhood memories of your immigrant father, missing his Swiss homeland, your brother dying of polio as a child, and to your becoming a caretaker for your husband before he died in 2020.
对痛苦没有耐心的文化中。我们可能就是那种文化。你在书中写到了你自己的损失经历，从你童年时期对移民父亲怀念瑞士家乡的记忆，到你弟弟童年时死于小儿麻痹症，再到你在丈夫2020年去世前成为他的照护者。

24
00:16:55.000 --> 00:17:28.000
How have these personal experiences shaped how you think about loss today? from the beginning and the earliest loss was my little brother who died of polio the summer before the SOC vaccine came out. My view already back then was that I didn't have to get over it. But then I was a bit of a rebel right then already. But I lived in a Swiss immigrant community, New Orleans, Wisconsin, where everybody came from, in that case, Switzerland.
这些个人经历是如何塑造你今天对损失的看法的？从一开始，最早的损失是我弟弟，他在索尔克疫苗问世前的那个夏天死于小儿麻痹症。那时我的观点就是，我不必“走出来”。但那时我已经有点叛逆了。但我生活在一个瑞士移民社区，威斯康星州的新奥尔良，每个人都来自那里，在这种情况下是瑞士。

25
00:17:28.000 --> 00:18:09.000
They were all homesick. I lived in an environment of homesickness in the village, but also in my home. So I was kind of accustomed to the fact that you don't cut bonds. You remember them, even though I had the sad version of it, a little heavy on the sadness side, I thought. But therefore, the community, I think, was perhaps advanced at that time in this regard, in that nobody ever told me I should get over it. there was always remembering class reunions would remember my dead brother, his classmates.
他们都患有思乡病。我生活在一个充满思乡之情的村庄环境里，在家里也是如此。所以我习惯了这样一个事实：你不会切断纽带。你会记住他们，尽管我觉得我有悲伤的版本，在悲伤方面有点沉重。但因此，我认为那个社区在当时这方面可能比较先进，因为从来没有人告诉我应该“走出来”。总是会有纪念活动，同学聚会会纪念我死去的弟弟，他的同学们也会。

26
00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:51.000
And so I perhaps was lucky to live in an environment as I was growing up where there was no closure. You remembered people every memorial day. You went to the gravesites and remembered them and so on. Today it's a different story as I live in more urban environment now. and corporate ideas that, you know, we'll give you a week, three days maybe to grieve and then come back and be productive. That assumes you get over it. And the thing is, you don't need to get over it. Even with a clear-cut death, with a death certificate, with funeral after, and all the appropriate rituals,
所以我可能很幸运，在成长过程中生活在一个没有“终结”的环境中。你会在每个阵亡将士纪念日记住人们。你会去墓地纪念他们等等。今天情况不同了，因为我现在生活在更城市化的环境中。以及企业理念认为，你知道，我们会给你一周，也许三天时间去悲伤，然后回来继续保持高效。这假设你已经“走出来”了。问题是，你不需要走出来。即使是明确的死亡，有死亡证明，有葬礼，以及所有适当的仪式，

27
00:18:51.000 --> 00:19:24.000
you don't need to get over it. You can live with grief. and that's recommended. And Bonano and other researchers say, and Nimar say there's oscillation, that it ebbs and flows, grief ebbs and flows, or comes in and out. And over time, those ebbs and flows, oscillations will be farther apart. But they're never gone. There's never closure. So the latest research certainly is in the idea of living.
你也不需要走出来。你可以与悲伤共存，这是值得推荐的。博纳诺和其他研究人员说，尼马尔说存在“振荡”，悲伤会起伏，或者进进出出。随着时间的推移，那些起伏、振荡会变得越来越远。但它们永远不会消失。从来没有“终结”。所以最新的研究肯定在于“与损失共存”的理念，而不是“走出来”。然后在书中，我写到了弗洛伊德事实上在给朋友的私人信件中传达的信息与他在学术著作中传达的信息不同，因为当客户对他说：“我很遗憾你的女儿去世了。”她在1920年死于流感。他说：“不，她还在这里。”他拍了拍他的背心口袋，里面一定有她的纪念品。现在，他从未在学术著作中告诉我们这一点。他谈到了“脱离”，我谈到了更多库布勒-罗斯和其他人，他们学术上写一种方式，个人写另一种方式。

28
00:19:24.000 --> 00:20:16.000
with loss, not getting over it. And then in the book, I write about the fact that Freud, in fact, gave us a different message in his personal letters to his friends than he did in his academic writings because when the client said to him, I'm sorry about your daughter who died. She died of the flu in 1920. And he said, no, she's still here. and he patted his vest pocket, wherein he must have had some momentum of her. Now, he never told us that in his academic writings. He talked about detachment, and I talk about more Kubler-Ross and others who write one way academically and write another way personally.
库布勒-罗斯在生命尽头，在她中风九次之后，抱歉，是多次中风，但活了九年无法行走。她说，死亡并不是那五个阶段，她本意是那样的，它不是线性的，它是混乱的。这对她来说很混乱，她对此感到非常愤怒。所以我们需要重新审视我们历史上的那些在个人著作中改变了想法的阶段。我写这本书更个人化，因为我想确保我在个人和专业上尽可能一致。这令人钦佩。我很感激你讲述那个故事。谢谢。对于许多年轻的美国人来说，大流行病是他们第一次经历生存威胁和大规模的社区损失。

29
00:20:16.000 --> 00:21:11.000
Kubler-Ross in the end of her life after she had nine strokes, many strokes, excuse me, but lived for nine years not being able to. walk. She said that dying was not the five stages, which she meant them to be, that it wasn't linear, that it's messy. And it was messy for her, and she was angry about it a lot. So we need to take another look at our historical stages who changed their minds in their personal writings. And I wrote this book more personally because I wanted to make sure I was congruent personally and professionally as much as I could. That's admirable. I appreciate you telling that story. Thank you. For many younger Americans, the pandemic was their first experience for existential threat and a communal large-scale loss.
现在，前几代人经历了经济大萧条、世界大战。人们过去在生命早期经历的损失要多得多。妇女通常死于分娩，或者儿童死于不再致命的传染病，因为我们有了更好的药物和疫苗。但你认为大流行病改变了当今年轻人对风险、不确定性和损失的看法吗？你认为这会对他们未来处理损失和悲伤的方式产生持久的影响吗？我想是的。现在下结论还为时过早。但我预测，经过这两年的动荡和对我们正常生活方式的颠覆，将会发生变化。我们已经看到了做事方式的变化。我对年轻人充满信心，因为他们比成年人，或者我们越老越有韧性。孩子们也很有韧性。他们会记住这一点，就像我记得

30
00:21:11.000 --> 00:22:16.000
Now, earlier generations live through a major depression, world wars. People used to experience much more loss a lot earlier in life. Women commonly died in childbirth or children died of communicable diseases that are no longer fatal because we have much better medicine and vaccines. But do you think that the pandemic has changed the way that the younger people today are thinking about risk, uncertainty, and loss? And do you think this will have lasting effects about how they approach loss and grief going forward? I think so. It's too soon to tell. But I predict there is going to be change as a result of this two years of turmoil and upheaval into our normal way of life. We already see changes and how we do things. And I have great faith in young people for being resilient, more resilient than adults. are or the older we get. And children are resilient too. They will remember this as I remembered
小儿麻痹症流行一样，就像我记得大萧条一样，就像我作为一个孩子记得第二次世界大战一样。我感到害怕，通过观察父母的脸，我会知道该有多害怕。但在某种程度上，那教会了我们什么，以及现在将教会年轻人什么，我认为，是你不能总是如愿以偿。你不能总是让世界按照你想要的方式运转。有悲剧。有困难时期。你知道吗？我们可以度过难关。我认为今天的年轻人可能会学到的是，他们度过了难关。好的。我和不少错过了大学毕业典礼、在没有说再见的情况下立即与朋友断绝联系的人谈过，等等，等等。

31
00:22:16.000 --> 00:23:08.000
the polio epidemic, as I remembered the Depression, and as I remembered World War II as a child. I was afraid by watching my parents' faces, I would know how afraid to be. But in a way, what that taught us and what will teach the young people now, I think, is that you can't always have your way. You can't always have the world be the way you want it to be. There are tragedies. There are difficult times. And you know what? We can get through them. And I think what young people today may learn is that they got through this. Okay. I talked to quite a few of them who missed college graduations who were cut off from friends immediately without saying bye, et cetera, et cetera.
但他们对此有一种我发现非常成熟的哲学回应。他们找到了其他方式与无法在现实中告别的朋友告别。他们知道他们未来可能会有我们所有人参加过的最好的同学聚会。他们变得更强大了。因为这个。也许我谈论的这一代人，这是他们第一次遇到事情不顺心的时候，对他们中的许多人来说。当然，也有例外。

32
00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:43.000
But they have a philosophic response to it that I find very mature. They found other ways to say goodbye to friends. They couldn't say goodbye to in reality. That they know they will have probably the best class reunions any of us have ever had down the line. They got stronger. because of it. And perhaps this generation I'm talking about, this is the first thing when things didn't go right for them, for many of them. Of course, there were exceptions.
我意识到了这一点。但我们有一段时间的育儿风格，我相信这干扰了年轻人压力管理的发展，父母会确保他们的孩子不感到痛苦。嗯，感到压力并能够应对它会教会你，你比你想象的要强大。这对你余生来说是非常宝贵的一课。我可以在现在的许多年轻人身上看到这一点。他们会因此变得更强大。并不是我希望任何人经历这些，但一旦逆境发生，而你在其中表现出韧性，你像风暴中的树一样弯曲，但你又站了起来。你在经历这些之后，在心理上会变得更强大。

33
00:23:43.000 --> 00:24:37.000
I realized that. But we've had a parenting style for a while that, that I believe has interfered with the stress management development of the young people, whereby parents would make sure their kids weren't distressed. Well, being stressed and being able to cope with it teaches you that you are stronger than you think. And that's a very valuable lesson for the rest of your life. And I can see that in many of the young people now. They will be stronger for it. Not that I wish this on anyone, but once adversity happens and you were resilient in it, you bent like a tree and a storm, but you got up again. You are stronger psychologically for having been through that.
那么，在你的职业生涯中写下并观察了这么多关于悲伤和损失的内容后，你认为自己基本上是一个乐观主义者还是悲观主义者？哦，我是个乐观主义者。我不认为自己是悲伤专家，因为那样的专家太多了。我认为自己是损失和损失细微差别的专家，是不同种类的损失及其造成的压力的专家。我从压力领域开始，这本质上是我的出身。模棱两可的损失不是一种关注病理的医学模型。它是一种关注韧性的压力模型。因此，并且来自一个在大萧条时期经历过艰难困苦的家庭，然后，正如我所说，第二次世界大战，看到人们在之后站起来。顺便说一句，这些都是可怕的事情。现在有了大流行病，我再次看到人们以一种比我以前见过的更成熟的方式，特别是在年轻人身上。

34
00:24:37.000 --> 00:25:42.000
So after writing and observing so much about grief and loss during your career, do you consider yourself basically an optimist or a pessimist about the human condition? Oh, I'm an optimist. And I don't consider myself a grief expert because there's so many of them. I consider myself an expert on loss and the nuances of loss, the different kinds of loss and the stress it causes. I started out in the stress field and that's essentially where I come from. Ambiguous loss is not a medical model focused on pathology. It's a stress model focused on resilience. As a result of that, And having come from a family of hard times during the Depression and then, as I said, World War II, and seeing people, you know, stand up after that. And these were terrible things, by the way. And now with the pandemic, again, I see people in a way that they have more maturity than I've ever seen before, in the young people especially.
在其他人身上，他们以非常有创造力的方式应对。确实，有些人没有，因为有些人相信二元思维。当然，大流行病的科学，科学从来不是二元的。它总是基于概率。他们想要一个干净的答案。他们想要“终结”。我处于危险之中吗？我戴口罩还是不戴？

35
00:25:42.000 --> 00:26:12.000
And in other people, they've coped in very creative ways. Indeed, some have not because some people believe in binary thinking. And, of course, the science of the pandemic, science is never binary. It's always based on probability. And they wanted a clean answer. They wanted closure. Am I in danger or not? Do I wear a mask or do I not?
病毒是真的还是假的？所以我们看到一部分人口需要绝对的思维，更像是“终结”。他们进行二元思考，你知道，你要么和我在一起，要么不在一起。要么死，要么活。要么安全，要么不安全。那种思维方式现在行不通。顺便说一句，这些人不是乐观主义者。所以我认为如果你能以一种更有韧性的方式思考，如果你能以一种更有韧性的方式行动，

36
00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:41.000
Is the virus real or is it not? So we saw a part of the population that needs absolute thinking, more like closure. They think in binaries, you know, you're either with me or you're not. It's either dead or alive. It's either safe or it's not safe. That way of thinking didn't work right now. And these are not optimists, by the way. So I think if you can think in a more resilient way, if you can act in a more resilient way,
如果你能以一种更有韧性的方式行动，灵活变通，但从中学习，每次都变得更强大，你也会变得乐观，因为这意味着你可以应对生活中出现的几乎任何事情，亲人的死亡、事故、失业。我并不希望任何人经历这些，但顺便说一句，死亡是不可避免的。如果只是你的父母变老并最终去世。所以生活会带来损失。我们练习保持韧性将会得到回报。正如我在书中写的那样，它会让我们知道，

37
00:26:41.000 --> 00:27:26.000
if you can act in a more resilient way, flexible, but learning from it, growing stronger each time, you will also be optimistic because what that means is you can cope with most anything that comes along in life, the death of your loved ones, accidents, loss of a job. I don't wish that on anyone, but by the way, death is inevitable. if only it's your parents getting older and eventually dying. And so life will bring its losses. And our practice with being resilient will pay off. It will let us know, as I wrote in the book,
你可以被打倒，但你可以再次站起来。好吧，博斯博士，我认为这是一个美妙的希望信息。我很感激你今天把它带给我们的听众。我希望他们学到了一些东西，这将帮助他们度过这些艰难的时期，我认为这对我们许多人来说都很困难。所以感谢你今天加入我们。我的荣幸。你可以在我们的网站 speakingofpsychology.org 上找到《心理学谈话》之前的剧集

